Each request writes one \fBforward\fR line to the access log with the client address, user, method, destination, status, bytes and duration. Counters: \fBforward_requests\fR, \fBforward_tunnels\fR, \fBforward_tunnels_open\fR, \fBforward_denied\fR, \fBforward_auth_failed\fR, \fBforward_rejected\fR, \fBforward_errors\fR, \fBforward_bytes_in\fR, \fBforward_bytes_out\fR; \fBxproxy_forward_*\fR metrics. The policy and the users file reload; the address and TLS settings need a restart like every listener.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns (kind: dns)"
.PP
A \fBkind: dns\fR listener is a forwarding DNS proxy on the listener address over UDP and TCP. Queries are answered from a bounded cache when they can be, refused or blocked by policy, and otherwise forwarded to the upstream resolvers with a fresh transaction id on a fresh socket (random source port) per query; the answer must echo the id and the question. A truncated UDP answer is retried over TCP to the upstream, and an answer larger than the client\(aqs UDP size (512 bytes or its EDNS advertisement, honoured up to 1232 bytes per RFC 9715 so a spoofed source cannot draw a large reply) is truncated so the client retries over TCP. Only one question per query and the QUERY opcode are handled (FORMERR and NOTIMP otherwise); responses arriving as queries and packets from banned clients are dropped. A dns listener takes \fBaddress\fR, \fBdns\fR and optionally \fBtls\fR; bans and the global connection limits apply to TCP clients as on every listener. The policy, upstreams and cache bounds reload (the cache is kept); the address needs a restart.
.PP
With \fBtls\fR (certificates only, no ACME) the listener is encrypted: no plain UDP is bound, the TCP port serves DNS over TLS (RFC 7858, ALPN \fBdot\fR or none) and DNS over HTTPS (RFC 8484, ALPN \fBh2\fR or \fBhttp/1.1\fR) at \fBdoh_path\fR, chosen per connection by the negotiated protocol. The same policy, cache and counters serve both; \fBqueries_dot\fR and \fBqueries_doh\fR count them, and \fBxproxyctl tls\fR shows the certificate. Run a plain listener on 53 and an encrypted one on 853 (and 443 when browsers should use it) side by side.
.PP
.TS
