.PP
Manual bans (\fBxproxyctl ban\fR) accept addresses, CIDRs no wider than /8 (IPv4) or /32 (IPv6) and fingerprints as \fBja4:<fp>\fR; loopback and unspecified addresses are refused. A network overlapping an exempt range is never banned, by trigger, operator or peer; a fingerprint ban is not applied to clients in exempt ranges. Fingerprint bans apply at the request stage (the fingerprint is known after the TLS handshake), not at accept; at most 4096 are held.
.SH "waf"
.PP
Present means enabled. Routes without a \fBwaf\fR block use \fBdefault_mode\fR and \fBdefault_profile\fR. Profiles compile at load and at reload; a broken rule set fails the reload.
.PP
