A honeypot route answers with a decoy that looks like the real thing (a WordPress login, a leaked \fB.env\fR, a \fB.git/config\fR) and records the client: a \fBhoneypot\fR security event with the full request line, a mark on the address for \fBmark\fR so that its later requests on every route are logged with \fBhoneypot_marked: true\fR and reach filters as \fBInfo.HoneypotMarked\fR, and a count towards the \fBhoneypot\fR ban reason. Nothing is proxied. Put honeypots on paths no legitimate client uses.
.PP
